00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:07.300
Let's look in this video at the process of how supervised learning works.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.540
Supervised learning algorithm will input a dataset and then what exactly does it do and

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:12.700
what does it output?

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:15.580
Let's find out in this video.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:20.900
Recall that a training set in supervised learning includes both the input features, such as

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:26.060
the size of the hulls, and also the output targets, such as the price of the hulls.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:30.980
The output targets are the right answers to the model we'll learn from.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:35.740
To train the model, you feed the training set both the input features and the output

00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:40.140
targets to your learning algorithm.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:45.180
Then your supervised learning algorithm will produce some function.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.860
We'll write this function as lowercase f, where f stands for function.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:55.580
Historically, this function used to be called a hypothesis, but I'm just going to call

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:58.500
it a function f in this class.

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:09.180
And the job of f is to take a new input x and output an estimate or prediction, which

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:16.340
I'm going to call y hat, and is written like the variable y with this little hat symbol

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:18.420
on top.

00:01:18.420 --> 00:01:25.780
In machine learning, the convention is that y hat is the estimate or the prediction for

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:27.940
y.

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.940
The function f is called the model.

00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:39.060
X is called the input or the input feature, and the output of the model is the prediction

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:41.060
y hat.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:45.580
The model's prediction is the estimated value of y.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:52.140
When the symbol is just the letter y, then that refers to the target, which is the

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:55.700
actual true value in the training set.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.500
In contrast, y hat is an estimate.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:01.100
It may or may not be the actual true value.

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:06.100
Well, if you're helping your client to sell their hulls, well, the true price of

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:09.140
their hulls is unknown until they sell it.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:15.180
So your model f, given the size, outputs a price which is the estimated, that is the

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:19.580
prediction of what the true price will be.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:25.900
Now when we design a learning algorithm, a key question is how are we going to

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.380
represent the function f?

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:35.460
Or in other words, what is the math formula we're going to use to compute f?

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:39.760
For now, let's stick with f being a straight line.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:47.740
So your function can be written as f subscript w comma b of x equals, I'm going

00:02:47.740 --> 00:02:52.220
to use w times x plus b.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:58.980
I'll define w and b soon, but for now, just know that w and b are numbers, and

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:06.180
the values chosen for w and b will determine the prediction y hat based on the

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.380
input feature x.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:16.220
So this f w b of x means f is a function that takes x's input, and depending on

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:24.180
the values of w and b, f will output some value of a prediction y hat.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:31.300
As an alternative to writing this f w comma b of x, I'll sometimes just write f

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.580
of x without explicitly including w and b in the subscript.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:43.180
It's just a simpler notation, but means exactly the same thing as f w b of x.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:47.340
Let's plot the training set on a graph where the input feature x is on the

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:54.220
horizontal axis, and the output target y is on the vertical axis.

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:59.940
Remember the algorithm learns from this data and generates a best fit line like

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:02.460
maybe this one here.

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:12.060
This straight line is the linear function f w b of x equals w times x plus b.

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:20.780
Or more simply, we can drop w and b and just write f of x equals w x plus b.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:22.580
Here's what this function is doing.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:28.620
It's making predictions for the value of y using a straight line function of x.

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:33.740
So you may ask, why are we choosing a linear function where linear function is

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.700
just a fancy term for a straight line instead of some non-linear function like

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:40.700
a curve or a parabola?

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:46.020
Well, sometimes you want to fit more complex non-linear functions as well, like a

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:47.500
curve like this.

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:51.900
But since this linear function is relatively simple and easy to work with,

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:56.540
let's use a line as a foundation that will eventually help you to get to more

00:04:56.540 --> 00:05:00.180
complex models that are non-linear.

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:04.500
This particular model as a name is called linear regression.

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:09.300
More specifically, this is linear regression with one variable, with a

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:14.180
phrase one variable means that there's a single input variable or feature x,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:17.140
namely the size of the house.

00:05:17.140 --> 00:05:22.900
Another name for a linear model with one input variable is univariate linear

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:29.540
regression, where uni means one in Latin and where variate means variable.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:34.580
So univariate is just a fancy way of saying one variable.

00:05:34.580 --> 00:05:39.540
In a later video, you'll also see a variation of regression where you want to

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:43.940
make a prediction based not just on the size of a house, but on a bunch of other

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.780
things that you may know about the house, such as number of bedrooms and other

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:48.900
features.

